WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.000
Where does a fish keep its money?

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:06.320
In the river bank!

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.600
This dress costs a 'hundred bucks'.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.460
This phone costs '10,000 bucks'.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.660
We usually hear people describing money, as bucks right?

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:19.620
But ever wondered how this slang for money originated?

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.220
Curious?

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.500
Time to find out.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.040
First of all,

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.380
can you tell me what buck literally means?

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.580
It usually refers to a male deer!

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.480
But what does Deer have to do with Money?

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:42.980
For this let us move back to America of the early 18th century.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:46.180
At that time, the US dollar was not invented,

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:50.960
and thus people traded in the form of goods or commodities.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:55.140
This kind of trade is referred to as the Barter system.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:59.280
The simplest example would be exchange of a boat for a bicycle.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.780
Or exchange of bread for butter and so on.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.060
In some parts of America,

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:09.660
Deerskin was one of the most highly valued commodity.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.640
This was usually due to its usefulness in clothing and moccasins,

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:18.780
and also because of its high quality.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:22.600
Therefore, trade was usually carried in the form of deerskin

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.060
which was referred to as buckskin.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:30.080
People at that time used to value things in terms of buckskin.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.100
For instance, 10 kilograms of rice for three buckskin,

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:38.700
a pair of moccasins for five buckskin and so on.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:43.060
One recorded example of this, is where a cask of whiskey

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.500
was traded by the European settlers to the native Americans

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.800
in exchange of five buckskins.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:54.620
As time passed, buckskin was shortened to bucks.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.120
Around the year 1792,

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.180
US Dollars were introduced,

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.920
which soon became the main medium of exchange.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.420
Although people stopped trading in buckskins,

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.900
the word BUCK still seemed to prevail in the minds of the people

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.120
as a reference for money.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.780
So this is how the slang 'Buck' for money

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:18.540
originated in the US, and then spread out worldwide.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:20.900
So this was about the 'BUCK'.

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:24.080
The word 'buck' is just a slang as you know now,

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:27.380
but every nation has its own currency.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:30.300
For example, US has Dollars,

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:31.740
Japan has Yen,

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.760
India has Rupee and so on...

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:36.340
On these lines,

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:41.500
have you ever wondered why different nations have different currencies?

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.040
Why can't there be a single currency worldwide?

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:49.420
Are there plans to build a single currency globally?

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:53.440
Well... this one is your Kyu Box to explore.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.440
Let us know your findings in the comment section.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.660
